Rem Capability: keys
Rem ==================================================================
Rem keys.shoddy — Shoddy standard library: physical-key classification.
Rem
Rem The keyboard-classification layer scribbler.shoddy left for later. A
Rem ScribblerKeyDown/Up event carries a raw ScribblerKeys code; ClassifyKey
Rem folds that number into a friendly GameKey so programs never compare
Rem against magic numbers. This is the PHYSICAL-key question ("the Left
Rem arrow went down", "the Q key went down") — what hotkeys, navigation
Rem and game controls want. The CHARACTER question ("the user produced a
Rem q") is separate and belongs to ScribblerTyped, layout/shift/IME
Rem already applied.
Rem
Rem Standalone: includes nothing. Constructor names avoid vt100.shoddy's
Rem KeyUp/KeyDown and the Left/Right string builtins, so a program may
Rem include this alongside either. The codes below are the stable
Rem ScribblerKeys table documented in scribbler.shoddy: letters 'A'..'Z'
Rem sit on 65..90, digits '0'..'9' on 48..57.
Rem ==================================================================

Type GameKey = ArrowLeft | ArrowRight | ArrowUp | ArrowDown | SpaceBar | EnterKey | EscKey | TabKey | BackKey | CharKey(Code) | OtherKey(Code)

Def ClassifyKey(code As Number) As GameKey
    Select Case code
        Case 262
            ArrowLeft()
        Case 263
            ArrowRight()
        Case 264
            ArrowUp()
        Case 265
            ArrowDown()
        Case 32
            SpaceBar()
        Case 13
            EnterKey()
        Case 27
            EscKey()
        Case 9
            TabKey()
        Case 8
            BackKey()
        Case 48 To 57
            CharKey(code)
        Case 65 To 90
            CharKey(code)
        Case Else
            OtherKey(code)

Rem The character a CharKey stands for ("A".."Z", "0".."9"); "" for the
Rem named keys and anything unmapped.
Def KeyGlyph(k As GameKey) As String
    Select Case k
        Case CharKey(c)
            Chr(c)
        Case Else
            ""

Rem True when k is the letter or digit whose glyph is s (case-insensitive:
Rem physical letters report uppercase, so IsKey(k, "q") matches the Q key).
Def IsKey(k As GameKey, s As String) As Boolean
    KeyGlyph(k) = Upper(s)
